# Working rules

## Verification

Verify that every file edit persisted, by re-reading the file or with
`git diff`, before reporting a task as done.

## Knowledge vault

Long-term memory lives in the Obsidian vault at `~/.claude/vault`. With Claude
Code, every project's auto-memory directory is linked to its `memory/` folder,
so recalled facts are the same in every repository. Codex and OpenCode get the
memory index (`memory/MEMORY.md`) loaded at session start and read the rest of
the vault directly; without auto-memory they save a new fact themselves, as one
file in `memory/` plus its line in `memory/MEMORY.md`. Read
`~/.claude/vault/CLAUDE.md` before writing anything into the vault and follow
its rules.

## Project rules come first

The `CLAUDE.md`, `README` and tool configuration of the project you work in
override everything general in this file: languages, conventions, how to lint,
test and run it.

## Working together

- Keep the user informed with short status updates.
- If a task is unclear, ask before starting.
- Never commit, push or open a pull request before the user has tested; then
  offer the commit.

## Coding tasks without a team

Tools without sub-agent teams follow the same quality path on their own:

1. Trace first when the change touches derived values, code that is extended in
   several places, or timing-sensitive code. Name the chain with file:line.
2. Implement the smallest change that solves the task, in the style of the
   surrounding code.
3. Review your own diff as a separate step, as strictly as a reviewer would.
4. Verify in two gates. Gate 1 (the project's linters, type checks and the tests
   covering the change) always runs. Gate 2 (full suite, restarts, migrations,
   anything disruptive) only after asking the user.
5. Report what was done, which gates ran and what remains unverified.
